# Shell scripts must be LF -- a CRLF `#!/usr/bin/env bash` script fails to
# parse on Linux/macOS (found empirically: sim/run_avrora.sh checked out
# with CRLF line endings threw a bash syntax error on this project's own
# elif branches). Every other tracked text file may still carry whatever
# line ending Windows checkouts have used so far; this only forces the
# one file type where the wrong ending is a functional bug, not a style
# issue.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Recorded inputs of the canonical Phase B experiment. The manifest at
# sim/fixtures/phase_b/manifest.json pins each of these by SHA-256, and those
# hashes were taken on Windows, so they are hashes of the CRLF byte sequence.
# git stores them LF (core.autocrlf=input), which means a Linux or macOS
# checkout would otherwise materialise a different byte sequence and fail the
# provenance check on a file nobody had touched -- pinning the canonical
# result to the machine that produced it. Forcing the checkout ending
# reproduces the recorded bytes everywhere, which is what the manifest is
# actually asserting. Found during the milestone-6 hardening pass; the
# alternative, rewriting the manifest to LF hashes, would have destroyed the
# provenance rather than preserved it.
cost_table.toml text eol=crlf
models/tiny_classifier_golden_input.txt text eol=crlf
models/tiny_classifier_golden_output.txt text eol=crlf
